C++ tests for the planner port
==============================

Two suites, run separately by PlatformIO (see platformio.ini):

    pio test -e native                    both
    pio test -e native -f test_contract   properties, invariants, scaling laws
    pio test -e native -f test_parity     bit-equality against the TypeScript

    ./.pio/build/native/program.exe -s    assertion counts; pio hides them

They answer different questions and have different lifespans. The parity suite
fails on any optimisation, correct or not, so it is scaffolding with a known end
date; the contract suite is what survives it.

Generated reference data — NOT in git
-------------------------------------

test/data/*.txt is generated and gitignored. The GENERATORS are tracked, under
web/test/port/cppRef*.test.ts, and are the only definition of these files. They
are large (discretize_ref.txt alone is 10 MB), fully reproducible, and would
otherwise be re-committed on every fixture change.

Generate all of them:

    cd web && GEN_CPP_REF=1 npx vitest run test/port

Without GEN_CPP_REF=1 the generators skip, so they cost nothing in a normal
vitest run.

Every test that reads one of these files fails with the exact command to
regenerate it, so an ungenerated checkout says so rather than passing over no
data. Two kinds:

    *_ref.txt          inputs AND expected outputs. Only the parity suite reads
                       these.
    svg_fixtures.txt   real artwork geometry, INPUT ONLY — the repaired subpaths
                       from the SVG parser and enforceC1, neither of which is in
                       the port's scope. The C++ flattens and constrains them
                       itself. Read by the CONTRACT suite, so that suite needs
                       one generator run too on a fresh checkout.

Check these files with is_open(), never good(): a default-constructed
std::ifstream has no error flags set, so good() returns true for a stream that
was never opened. testbits::openRef now sets failbit on a miss so both work, but
the habit is what matters — every caller here used to check good(), and deleting
test/data produced five confusing assertion failures about case counts and not
one about a missing file.

Layout
------

    main.cpp           doctest runner. In the test_dir ROOT, which PlatformIO
                       shares into every suite.
    support/           bits.h (hex + openRef), curves.h (curve fixtures),
                       quality.h + machine.h (the shipped config, duplicated by
                       hand on purpose — see their headers), svgfix.h
    data/              generated; see above
    test_contract/     one file per stage
    test_parity/       one file per stage

docs/port_workflow.md is the procedure these were built with, and
docs/planner_audit.md is what they found.
